    "markdown": "## **Should, shouldn’t**: Form\n\n![Grammar structure chart for the modal verb 'should' showing positive form 'should go', negative form 'shouldn't go', question form 'Should go?', and short answers for A2 English learners.](/uploads/grammar/a2/should-shouldnt/img-1.png)\n\n#### The same form for all persons\n\nThe form of **should** is the same for all persons:\n\n-   *I/you/he/she/it/we/you/they **should** see a specialist.* \n\n#### **Should(n’t)** + infinitive\n\n**Should** is a modal verb, and all modal verbs are followed by an [infinitive](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/infinitives-and-gerunds/) (without **to**).\n\n-   *You **should to listen** to me. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/should-shouldnt/img-2.png)* \n-   *You **should listen** to me. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/should-shouldnt/img-3.png)* \n\n#### **Should not= shouldn’t**\n\nThe negative form of **should** is **should not** or **shouldn’t**.\n\n-   *You **should not be** here.* = *You **shouldn’t be** here.* \n\n#### Questions\n\nTo [ask questions](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/asking-questions-in-english/), we use **should** + subject + infinitive. We don’t use the verb **do** to ask questions.\n\n-   ***Do** we **should** call him? ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/should-shouldnt/img-4.png)* \n-   ***Should we call** him? ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/should-shouldnt/img-5.png)* \n\n## **Should, shouldn’t**: Use\n\n#### Giving advice\n\nWe use **should** or **shouldn’t** to give somebody advice and to say what is or isn’t the right thing to do.\n\n-   *You look tired. You **should have** some rest.* \n-   *He **shouldn’t drive** so fast. He’ll have an accident one day.* \n\n#### **I think you should …**\n\nWe often say **I think … should** **…** to give somebody advice.\n\n-   ***I think you should buy** a new pair of shoes for the party.* \n-   ***I think we should go** home; it’s very late.* \n\n🔍 **Note** that in a negative sentence, we often say **I don’t think… should…** (NOT **I think… shouldn’t…**)\n\n-   ***I think you shouldn’t call** her now; she’s very upset. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/should-shouldnt/img-6.png)* \n-   ***I don’t think you should call** her now; she’s very upset.* *![](/uploads/grammar/a2/should-shouldnt/img-7.png)*\n\nWe often say **Do you think … should …** to ask for advice.\n\n-   ***Do you think I should look** for another apartment?*\n\n#### **Ought to, ought not to**\n\nWe can also use **ought to** instead of **should** and **ought not to** instead of **shouldn’t**.\n\n-   *You **ought to have** some rest.*\n-   *He **ought not to drive** so fast.*",
